narrative:
## Parsing Configuration Values

Configuration files mix numbers, text, and boolean values. An enum with variant data captures all three in one type.

### The ConfigValue Enum

```xiom
enum ConfigValue {
  Number(n: Int),
  Text(s: Str),
  YesNo(b: Bool),
}
```

### Creating Config Values

```xiom
let port = ConfigValue.Number(8080);
let host = ConfigValue.Text("localhost");
let debug = ConfigValue.YesNo(true);
```

### Displaying Config Values

Each variant formats differently:

```xiom
fn ConfigValue.display(self) -> Str {
  match self {
    Number(n) => { return "number: " + n; }
    Text(s) => { return "text: " + s; }
    YesNo(b) => {
      if b { return "yes"; }
      return "no";
    }
  }
}
```

### Safely Extracting Numbers

Only `Number` variants have a numeric value. Others return a default:

```xiom
fn ConfigValue.as_number(self) -> Int {
  match self {
    Number(n) => { return n; }
    Text(_) => { return 0; }
    YesNo(b) => {
      if b { return 1; }
      return 0;
    }
  }
}
```

### Using Config Values

```xiom
port.display();       // "number: 8080"
host.display();       // "text: localhost"
debug.display();      // "yes"

port.as_number();     // 8080
host.as_number();     // 0 (not a number)
debug.as_number();    // 1 (true converts to 1)
```

